// RPlotPlot builds a scatter, line, or line-plus-points plot of `y` against `x`, like R's
// `plot(x, y, type = "p" | "l" | "b")`.
//
// `"p"` (the default) adds one scatter series with 4-pixel markers; `"l"` adds one 2-pixel
// line joining the points in input order; `"b"` adds the line and then unlabeled points on
// top (R's `"b"` leaves gaps in the line around each point; this draws it through them). The
// title and axis labels keep their defaults. `RPlot(x, y)` is `plot(x, y)`.
//
// # Example
//
//	const auto x = dm::seql(0.0, 4.0, 5);
//	auto plt = dm::Plot::plot(x, x * x, "b", "x squared");
//	std::print("{} series\n", plt.series().size());  // 2 series
//
// # References
//
//   - R `plot.default()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/plot.default.html
//
// Parameters:
//
//   - x: Horizontal coordinates, one per point.
//   - y: Vertical coordinates, paired with `x` by position.
//
// It returns A new plot holding the series.
//
// The error is an [*Error] of kind:
//
//   - [InvalidArgument] (C++ std::invalid_argument): If the coordinates are empty or differ in
//     length, or `type` is not one of the three codes.
//
// It uses the C++ defaults type_ = "p", label = "", color; [RPlotPlotWithType],
// [RPlotPlotWithLabel] and [RPlotPlotWithColor] pass more arguments.
//
// See also RPlot(std::vector<double>, std::vector<double>), points() and lines().
func RPlotPlot(x []float64, y []float64) (*RPlot, error) {
	return rosetta.Result[*RPlot](rosetta.Call(1048, x, y))
}

