// Polygon draws the closed polygon through the vertices (x[i], y[i]), like R's
// `polygon(x, y, col = color)`.
//
// The last vertex joins the first automatically. Filled polygons (the default) are painted
// with `color` (plus a 1-pixel outline in SVG; the raster fill uses the even-odd rule);
// unfilled ones draw a 2-pixel outline only (R's default is unfilled). Translucent colors work
// well
// for shaded regions such as confidence bands. Vertices count toward the axis ranges.
//
// # Example
//
//	auto plt = dm::Plot()
//	               .polygon({0.0, 1.0, 0.5}, {0.0, 0.0, 1.0}, dm::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 0.4})
//	               .polygon({0.2, 0.8, 0.8, 0.2}, {0.1, 0.1, 0.4, 0.4}, dm::RGB{0, 0, 0}, false);
//	std::print("{}\n", plt.series()[1].filled);  // false
//
// # References
//
//   - R `polygon()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/polygon.html
//
// Parameters:
//
//   - x: Vertex x coordinates, in order around the polygon.
//   - y: Vertex y coordinates, paired with `x`.
//
// It returns this plot, for chaining.
//
// The error is an [*Error] of kind:
//
//   - [InvalidArgument] (C++ std::invalid_argument): If the coordinates are empty or have
//     different lengths.
//
// It uses the C++ defaults color, filled = true; [RPlot.PolygonWithColor] and
// [RPlot.PolygonWithFilled] pass more arguments.
//
// See also pie() and segments().
func (r *RPlot) Polygon(x []float64, y []float64) (*RPlot, error) {
	return rosetta.Result[*RPlot](rosetta.Call(1067, r, x, y))
}

// PolygonWithColor draws the closed polygon through the vertices (x[i], y[i]), like R's
