// Image draws a grid of coloured rectangles, one per value of `z`, like R's `image(x, y, z)`.
//
// Cell (i, j) is centred on (x[i], y[j]) and reaches halfway to its neighbours (a half step
// beyond the outermost centres). Its colour is the palette entry for z[i][j] on a linear
// scale from `z_min` to `z_max`; NaN cells are left empty. Each cell is added as a filled
// polygon, so the grid should have at most tens of thousands of cells.
//
// # Example
//
//	auto plt = dm::graphics::RPlot::create().image({0.0, 1.0}, {0.0, 1.0, 2.0}, {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}});
//	std::print("{}\n", plt.series().size());  // 6
//
// # References
//
//   - R `image()`: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/image.html
//
// Parameters:
//
//   - x: Cell centres along x, increasing (at least 2).
//   - y: Cell centres along y, increasing (at least 2).
//   - z: Values, z[i][j] at (x[i], y[j]); z.size() == x.size() and each row has y.size() entries.
//
// It returns this plot, for chaining.
//
// The error is an [*Error] of kind:
//
//   - [InvalidArgument] (C++ std::invalid_argument): If the sizes do not match, x or y is not
//     increasing, or the palette is empty.
//
// It uses the C++ defaults palette = the zero value, zMin, zMax; [RPlot.ImageWithPalette],
// [RPlot.ImageWithZMin] and [RPlot.ImageWithZMax] pass more arguments.
//
// See also terrain_colors() and polygon().
func (r *RPlot) Image(x []float64, y []float64, z [][]float64) (*RPlot, error) {
	return rosetta.Result[*RPlot](rosetta.Call(1068, r, x, y, z))
}

