// DynamicTimeWarpingWork calls C++ datamunge::geometry::dynamic_time_warping_work.
//
// Compute unrestricted absolute-loss DTW and actual logical work without cost history.
// Owns the result; borrows finite inputs without mutation. No band, slope bound,
// step weighting, length normalization or fitted parameters. Finite ties choose
// diagonal, then advance_a, then advance_b. O(n*m) recurrence time, O(n*m+n+m)
// scratch/output, with only two double cost rows and n*m predecessor bytes.
// Complete input/geometry validation precedes allocation. Positive overflowing
// off-path costs remain infinity; only a nonfinite final optimum is rejected.
// Under ordinary double arithmetic the returned witness sums to the returned
// distance in forward order. Concurrent independent calls share no mutable state.
//
// # Example
//
// See DTWWork and DTWComputation for executed work examples.
//
// # References
//
//   - H. Sakoe and S. Chiba (1978), Dynamic programming algorithm optimization for
//     spoken word recognition, IEEE TASSP26(1):43-49. doi:10.1109/TASSP.1978.1163055.
//     This is the unrestricted scalar absolute-loss variant, without the paper's window/slope
//     rules.
//
// Parameters:
//
//   - a: Nonempty finite scalar sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
//   - b: Nonempty finite scalar sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
//
// It returns independent optimal result and counts, with no retained input/cost history.
//
// The error is an [*Error] of kind:
//
//   - [InvalidArgument] (C++ std::invalid_argument): For empty or nonfinite input.
//   - [LengthError] (C++ std::length_error): For geometry/index/container bounds.
//   - [ArithmeticError] (C++ std::overflow_error): If the final minimum is not finite double.
//   - [AllocationError] (C++ std::bad_alloc): If scratch or result storage cannot be allocated.
//
// See also dynamic_time_warping() and dynamic_time_warping_trace().
func DynamicTimeWarpingWork(a []float64, b []float64) (*DTWComputation, error) {
	return rosetta.Result[*DTWComputation](rosetta.Call(134, a, b))
}

