// TTestTwoSample calls C++ datamunge::stats::t_test_two_sample.
//
// Independent two-sample Welch or pooled Student mean test, like R t.test().
//
// Welch uses unequal sample variances and Satterthwaite approximate df; normality alone does not
// make its reference exact. Pooled Student requires equal population variances and independent
// normal observations for its exact reference. CI estimates mean(x)-mean(y). O(n_x+n_y) work,
// inputs unchanged, no shared mutable state or omission.
//
// # Example
//
//	const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_two_sample({1,2,3},{4,5,6});
//	std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.estimate1); // 2
//
// # References
//
//   - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html
//
// Parameters:
//
//   - x: At least two finite observations in the first group.
//   - y: At least two finite independent observations in the second group.
//
// It returns statistic=t, parameter1=df, estimate1/2=group means, p_value and confidence bounds
// for their difference. One-sided bounds can be infinite.
//
// The error is an [*Error] of kind:
//
//   - [InvalidArgument] (C++ std::invalid_argument): Invalid sample sizes/values,
//     alternative/confidence, unrepresentable mean difference or zero/nonfinite combined standard
//     error.
//
// It uses the C++ defaults equalVariance = false, alternative = AlternativeTwoSided, confLevel
// = 0.95; [TTestTwoSampleWithEqualVariance], [TTestTwoSampleWithAlternative] and
// [TTestTwoSampleWithConfLevel] pass more arguments.
//
// See also [HypothesisTestResult].
func TTestTwoSample(x []float64, y []float64) (*HypothesisTestResult, error) {
	return rosetta.Result[*HypothesisTestResult](rosetta.Call(202, x, y))
}

