// FTestVariance calls C++ datamunge::stats::f_test_variance.
//
// F-test and interval for a ratio of variances, like R var.test().
//
// Uses sample variance(x)/sample variance(y). Independent normal populations give the
// F(df_x,df_y) reference; it is sensitive to nonnormality and is not a gatekeeper for choosing
// Welch versus pooled t tests. O(n_x+n_y) work/O(1) auxiliary storage; inputs unchanged, no
// omission/shared state.
//
// # Example
//
//	const auto f=dm::stats::f_test_variance({1,2,3},{2,4,6});
//	std::print("{:.2f}\n",f.estimate1); // 0.25
//
// # References
//
//   - https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/var.test.html
//
// Parameters:
//
//   - x: At least two finite first-sample values with positive finite sample variance.
//   - y: At least two finite independent second-sample values with positive finite sample
//     variance.
//
// It returns statistic=estimate1=variance ratio, parameter1/2=sample sizes minus one, p_value and
// ratio confidence bounds; one-sided interval ends at zero or infinity.
//
// The error is an [*Error] of kind:
//
//   - [InvalidArgument] (C++ std::invalid_argument): Too few/nonfinite observations,
//     nonpositive/nonfinite variances or invalid controls.
//
// It uses the C++ defaults alternative = AlternativeTwoSided, confLevel = 0.95;
// [FTestVarianceWithAlternative] and [FTestVarianceWithConfLevel] pass more arguments.
//
// See also [HypothesisTestResult].
func FTestVariance(x []float64, y []float64) (*HypothesisTestResult, error) {
	return rosetta.Result[*HypothesisTestResult](rosetta.Call(204, x, y))
}

